I think there’s an even deeper analogue to imaginary numbers here.

Complex numbers let mathematics leave a one-dimensional line and operate naturally in a richer space while still projecting back to real answers when needed.

A similar extension for programming would let computation leave a single execution trace and operate over a manifold of compatible states:

Program =
    Values
  + Constraints
  + Alternatives
  + Confidence
  + Provenance

Instead of asking, “What is the value of x?” the runtime asks, “What region of state space satisfies everything I currently know?” Computation then becomes progressively narrowing that region as new constraints arrive.

In that sense, the analogue of the imaginary axis isn’t “imaginary data.” It’s an additional computational dimension that allows programs to carry possibility, relationships, and latent structure alongside concrete values, then project back to a definite result only when required. That extra degree of freedom can make certain classes of reasoning—search, inference, planning, and semantic reconstruction—much more compositional, much as complex numbers simplify rotation and wave mechanics.